  These scripts read files from disk (fs.readFileSync / travellingFiles) and are development/build utilities (kystudio) — not production HTTP endpoints. See e.g. file reads at repo/kystudio/build/explore-chinese.js:105-116 and repo/kystudio/build/translate-extract.js:103-116.

- Server REST code (production entry points) do not evaluate attacker-controlled code strings:
  - Example controller package and code: repo/src/common-server/src/main/java/org/apache/kylin/rest/controller/NBasicController.java (typical REST controller/handler code). A manual inspection of server-side source tree did not reveal calls to javax.script.ScriptEngineManager, ScriptEngine.eval, GroovyShell, BeanShell, javax.tools.JavaCompiler, or similar script-compilation/eval APIs in production code.
    * Controller path: [repo/src/common-server/src/main/java/org/apache/kylin/rest/controller/NBasicController.java]

- javax.script references are present only in test files/annotations (not runtime code):
  - repo/src/common-server/src/test/java/org/apache/kylin/rest/controller/NBasicControllerTest.java shows javax.script.* only in a PowerMockIgnore annotation used for tests (not indicating runtime ScriptEngine use): [repo/src/common-server/src/test/java/org/apache/kylin/rest/controller/NBasicControllerTest.java:1-140]

- Additional scan: I searched the repository for common script-evaluation APIs (ScriptEngine, javax.script, GroovyShell, BeanShell, JavaCompiler, JS eval patterns) and did not find usage in the production server code paths; the only concrete matches for string-eval were the two kystudio build scripts cited above.

CONCLUSION / IMPACT RATIONALE:
- To fail this CWE-94 test we would need to show a production-reachable endpoint that accepts attacker-controlled text that is incorporated into a code/template string and then evaluated/interpreted at runtime (allowing the attacker to change syntax/behavior). No such dataflow was found in the repository. The only eval usage is build-time tooling operating on local source files; that is outside the test scope (not production-run nor attacker-controlled input).

If you want, I can run a more exhaustive automated search for additional eval-like patterns or list every grep result I used to reach this verdict.
